# Files whose line endings are load-bearing.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on the machine this project is developed on, so a
# fresh clone rewrites LF to CRLF. That is harmless for everything in this
# repository except the two files below, and for the Dockerfile it is fatal:
# fed CRLF, BuildKit answers
#
#   dockerfile parse error on line 3: unknown instruction: echo
#
# because a `\` line continuation followed by a carriage return is not a
# continuation. There are four of them in the layer that installs and
# version-gates ffmpeg, so a fresh Windows clone simply cannot build the image.
# Measured against a real build, not assumed.
#
# `.dockerignore` is pinned for a weaker reason and pinned anyway: BuildKit was
# tested and DOES strip the CR there, so a CRLF ignore file still keeps `.env`
# out. Depending on that stripping to keep seven secrets out of a published
# image is a thinner guarantee than not needing it.
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
# The deploy topology, pinned for the weaker `.dockerignore` reason: YAML and
# Caddy both tolerate CRLF today, and depending on that tolerance is a thinner
# guarantee than not needing it.
compose.yaml    text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf
